  :adr/doc_type "adr"
  :adr/source-format "edn"
  :adr/date "2026-07-19"
  :adr/status :accepted
  :adr/title "kotoba-lang/mrz: ICAO 9303 machine-readable-zone decode/checksum as pure data contracts"
  :adr/scope [:kotoba-lang/mrz]
  :adr/related ["orgs/kotoba-lang/ekyc" "orgs/kotoba-lang/aml" "orgs/kotoba-lang/identity" "orgs/kotoba-lang/num"]
  :adr/context
  "eKYC (electronic identity verification) came up while closing network-isekai's
   account-age-tier gap (ADR-0069 in that repo): kotoba-lang/ekyc and kotoba-lang/aml
   both already exist, but their own docstrings say plainly they carry no verification
   engine -- they are session/evidence-ledger substrates awaiting a real provider
   behind ekyc.adapters.provider/IEkycProviderClient. The owner explicitly chose to
   build a from-scratch verification engine in kotoba-lang rather than contract a
   real accredited vendor (Onfido/Jumio/etc). A prior planning pass (Explore + Plan
   subagents, this session) surveyed kotoba-lang for reusable CV/OCR/biometric
   infrastructure and found none -- kotoba-lang/inference + torch + num explicitly
   reject ONNX/TF.js/WebNN as portable foundations and have no vision-workload
   precedent, only an LLM-shaped GGUF/Gemma inference path.

   Given that, the highest-value, most honestly-buildable first slice is the
   Machine Readable Zone (MRZ) printed on ICAO Doc 9303-compliant travel/identity
   documents (passports and many national ID cards, issued by ~190 countries).
   Unlike face-match or document-authenticity detection -- genuinely hard,
   unsolved-by-us CV problems where a half-real implementation would create false
   confidence and enable real fraud -- the MRZ format itself is a PUBLIC,
   DETERMINISTIC, fully-specified standard: fixed-width TD1 (3x30) / TD2 (2x36) /
   TD3 (2x44) layouts, a fixed 44-glyph OCR-B alphabet, and a published 7-3-1
   weighted-mod-10 checksum algorithm per field plus a composite check digit.
   Decoding and validating MRZ TEXT (once it has been read, by any means, into a
   string) is something this repo can implement for real, with correctness
   verifiable against ICAO's own published worked example -- not a probabilistic
   claim, a deterministic one.

   The OPTICAL step (photograph -> MRZ text) is a different, much harder problem.
   MRZ's fixed font/zone/aspect-ratio narrows it considerably versus general
   document OCR, so a classical (non-neural) glyph-template-correlation matcher on
   kotoba-lang/num's tensor backend is a legitimate v1 approach for a pre-cropped,
   perspective-corrected zone image -- but has NOT been validated against any real
   photographed-document corpus (none exists in this workspace, and none is
   fabricated here). This ADR ships that piece as explicitly experimental (R1),
   distinct from the decode/checksum core (R2-target, ICAO-vector-verified)."
  :adr/decision
  "Add kotoba-lang/mrz, a portable .cljc library:

   - mrz.model -- TD1/TD2/TD3 field-width tables, the OCR-B character-to-digit
     mapping ICAO 9303 Part 3 defines (0-9 -> 0-9, A-Z -> 10-35, '<' -> 0).
   - mrz.checksum -- the 7-3-1 weighted mod-10 checksum, pure, one function over
     any character string + expected check digit. Verified against ICAO Doc 9303's
     own published TD3 worked example (document number L898902C3 -> check digit 6,
     DOB 740812 -> check digit 2, expiry 120415 -> check digit 9, personal number
     ZE184226B<<<<< -> check digit 1 -- each independently hand-verified against
     the algorithm before landing, not merely copied from a third party).
   - mrz.core -- parse-line / parse-td1 / parse-td2 / parse-td3 / validate: decode
     a raw MRZ string into named fields (document type, issuing state, names,
     document number, nationality, DOB, sex, expiry, personal number) and report
     per-field + composite checksum pass/fail. This is the load-bearing, R2-target
     surface: a caller supplying already-read MRZ text gets a real, deterministic
     validity verdict, honestly claimable across all ICAO-9303-issuing countries.
   - mrz.ports/IZoneOcr -- a host-injected protocol for the optical step (mirrors
     kotoba.webrtc.session's 'host injects the concrete capability' split already
     established as house convention). The kernel (mrz.core) never depends on it.
   - mrz.glyph + mrz.adapters.template-ocr -- an R1 EXPERIMENTAL IZoneOcr
     implementation: correlation-match against a bundled OCR-B 44-glyph template
     bank, running on kotoba-lang/num's tensor backend. Requires a pre-cropped,
     perspective-corrected MRZ zone image (no document detection, no zone
     localization, no perspective correction in v1 -- explicit follow-ups, not
     silently assumed). No accuracy claim is made or implied for this piece;
     MATURITY.md states plainly that real-photograph accuracy is unvalidated.

   No cross-kotoba-lang deps for mrz.model/checksum/core (pure data + pure
   functions); mrz.glyph depends on kotoba-lang/num for its tensor backend.
   :test/:lint aliases match house convention (cognitect test-runner + clj-kondo).

   This library does not itself implement ekyc.adapters.provider/IEkycProviderClient
   -- that composition happens in a later, separate kotoba-lang/ekyc-native-provider
   repo (per the session's approved plan) once mrz, face-liveness, and the
   deliberately-stubbed face-match all exist; mrz stays a standalone, reusable,
   ekyc-agnostic library, matching kotoba-lang/composer's precedent (ADR-2607031510)
   of not coupling a pure-data capability library to any one consumer's wiring."
  :adr/consequences
  ["Any eKYC/AML consumer in this workspace (the planned kotoba-lang/ekyc-native-provider, the planned cloud-itonami-isic-649 verification actor, or any future consumer) gets a real, ICAO-9303-conformant MRZ decode/validate primitive instead of re-deriving the checksum algorithm from scratch."
   "The optical (photo -> text) path is explicitly NOT claimed as production-ready or benchmarked -- MATURITY.md and README state this plainly, mirroring isekai.moderation / ekyc's own :non-adjudicating honesty convention, so a downstream consumer cannot mistake R1-experimental optical matching for the R2-target decode/checksum core."
   "Zone localization, perspective correction, and any real-photograph accuracy benchmark remain explicit, documented follow-ups -- not silently deferred or implied as done."
   "deps.edn: mrz.model/checksum/core have zero kotoba-lang dependencies (pure, embeddable anywhere .cljc runs); mrz.glyph depends on kotoba-lang/num, following the org's established torch+num tensor-backend convention rather than pulling in ONNX/TF.js/WebNN."]
  :adr/references [2607031510]}]
